# 🔒 Security & Permissions

## Roles
| | Visitor | Student | Admin |
|---|:-:|:-:|:-:|
| Open platform pages | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ |
| Upload a file (pending approval) | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ |
| See unapproved files | ✗ | ✗ | ✓ |
| Approve and delete | ✗ | ✗ | ✓ |
| See students and search them | ✗ | ✗ | ✓ |

## How protection is enforced
Real protection lives in Supabase (RLS policies), not in the browser. `supabase/schema.sql` creates:
- A `profiles` table (name, email, role), set to `student` automatically on sign-up
- An `is_admin()` function to check for admins
- Policies that stop students from approving, deleting, or seeing other users' data

## ⚠️ After running schema.sql
Review old policies, because a single permissive one is enough to leave the door open:
`select tablename, policyname, cmd, roles from pg_policies where tablename in ('submissions','objects','profiles');`
Drop any old policy that lets any signed-in user update or delete:
`drop policy "policy_name" on public.submissions;`

## Keys
- The publishable key is meant to be visible in the browser and is safe to keep in code
- The `service_role` key is secret: never put it in code or on GitHub

## Storage files
The bucket is public: anyone with a file link can open it. Do not store confidential material in it.

## Sign-up
- Confirm email is currently off (anyone can register with an email they don't own)
- To pause sign-ups: turn off Allow new users to sign up in Supabase
- Make an admin: `update public.profiles set role = 'admin' where email = '...';`